PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Core constraint unchanged: only a single listed method may be used, and it must produce BOTH (1) species IDs for every pollinator across 500 k frames and (2) per-image flower-feeding counts. Standard image-classification heads (EfficientNet/ResNet) output only a whole-image species label (or top-k); they supply no localization, instance counting, or interaction labels. This architectural gap rules out A–C and therefore E/F regardless of species count, training time, or the 10 cm/3 MP FOV. Only manual review (D) can generate both required outputs in one pass.

Round-1 split is stable: majority (colleagues 1,3,4,6,8,10–12,15,18–20) correctly reiterates the completeness failure of CNNs and treats post-processing as an extra unlisted method that violates the “only method used” wording. Minority (2,5,7,9,13,14,16,17) continues to claim A by (a) asserting 5 species suffice and (b) assuming trivial connected-component/threshold steps on non-insect pixels will magically supply counts; these steps are never mentioned in the option descriptions and would themselves constitute additional methods.

No new evidence alters the logic. Time figures remain secondary once functional adequacy is considered. Persistent open question: whether the problem implicitly allows unstated auxiliary CV routines (none is described). Current belief: answer remains unambiguously D.}

